===Etymology 1===
 {{root|en|ine-pro|4=*sóh₂wl̥}}From {{inh|en|enm|sonne}}, {{m|enm|sunne}}, from {{inh|en|ang|sunne}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*sunnā}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*sunnǭ}}, from [[heteroclitic]] [[inanimate]] {{inh|en|ine-pro||*sh₂wen-}}, oblique of {{der|en|ine-pro|*sóh₂wl̥|t=sun}}.

See also {{cog|stq|Sunne}}, {{cog|fy|sinne}}, {{cog|nds-de|Sünn}}, {{cog|nl|zon}}, {{cog|de|Sonne}}, {{cog|is|sunna}}; outside of Germanic, {{cog|cy|huan}}, {{cog|sa|स्वर्}}, {{cog|ae|𐬓𐬇𐬧𐬔}}).

Related to {{doublet|en|sol|id1=day|Sol|Surya|Helios|notext=1}}. More at {{m|en|solar}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{sense|proper noun|star which the Earth revolves around}} {{alter|en|Sun||capitalized}}
* {{alter|en|sonne|sunne||obsolete spelling}}

====Proper noun====
{{en-proper noun|head=the sun}}
[[File:Skylab Solar flare.jpg|thumbnail|The Sun photographed by Skylab 4.]]

# {{senseid|en|Q525}}The star that the [[Earth]] revolves around and from which it receives light and warmth.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=233|passage="I suppose I may have leave to do that!" Yes, she could do that, he said, but there was no road to that place; it lay east of the '''sun''' and west of the moon, and she could never find her way there.}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage='Twas early June, the new grass was flourishing everywheres, the posies in the yard—peonies and such—in full bloom, '''the sun''' was shining, and the water of the bay was blue, with light green streaks where the shoal showed.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* While the sun by tradition is typically regarded as masculine, the noun itself was originally feminine in grammatical gender.

